"Separates business decisions from the input data, reusable knowledge and authorities needed to make them.\n\nUse when: Agree the scope of a complex policy decision. Expose missing information or policy authority before implementing decision logic.\n\nAvoid when: The question concerns activity sequence or only one rule table.\n\nVisual grammar: Rectangles denote decisions; rounded shapes denote input data. Knowledge models and knowledge sources use distinct DMN symbols. Typed requirement arrows distinguish information, knowledge and authority.\n\nBuild: Name the final business decision and its required output. Decompose it into supporting decisions and identify necessary inputs. Attach reusable knowledge and authority, then specify each decision's logic separately.\n\nCheck: Requirement links use valid source and target element types. Decisions state outputs rather than procedural actions. No information dependency cycle prevents evaluating the decision.\n\nPitfalls: Drawing control-flow arrows between business tasks. Confusing a policy authority with the data used to apply it.\n\nExample: A loan eligibility decision depends on affordability, identity verification and application facts, with lending policy shown as its authority.\n\nAlternatives: DMN decision table, Influence diagram."
